        1. Install I2P Java, start router

            Follow [these instructions](https://i2p.net/en/downloads) to install I2P Java on your system, then start the router:
            ``` Bash
            sudo systemctl start i2p.service
            ```

            Alternatively, if you downloaded a binary:
            ``` Bash
            ./i2p/i2prouter start
            ```

        2. Create a SOCKS tunnel

            The SOCKS proxy tunnel is not enabled by default in I2P Java. Open the tunnel manager at `http://127.0.0.1:7657/i2ptunnel/`, click on `Tunnel Wizard`, select a client tunnel, select type `SOCKS 4/4a/5`, and set the port to `4447`.

        3. Create server tunnels for P2P and RPC

            Add two server tunnels with separate destinations, one for P2P on local port 18085 and one for RPC on local port 18089.

            From the same tunnel manager, click on `Tunnel Wizard` again, choose a server tunnel of type `Standard`, set the target host to `127.0.0.1`, and the target port to the desired number. Repeat this for both tunnels on a new setup, then save and start them. Check in the tunnel settings that they use different private key files. For an existing setup, keep the P2P tunnel and its key.

            This will create hidden service keys under your I2P config directory, usually `~/.i2p/` for a user install or `/var/lib/i2p/i2p-config/` for a system service install; you may want to back these up.

            Under "I2P Hidden Services", copy the Base32 address of the P2P tunnel for `anonymous-inbound` and the different address of the RPC tunnel for your wallets. This can take a couple of minutes on first startup. If your existing RPC tunnel shares the P2P key, back up the key, stop the RPC tunnel and disable its automatic startup. Leave that old tunnel in place: deleting it can move the shared key file. Create a new RPC tunnel with its own key, then update your wallets to its new address.

        4. Modify the config file

            Add the P2P address to your Monero [**config file** :link:{ title="Monerod Config" }]{{ config }}, enabling these options:
            ``` ini
            anonymous-inbound=yourp2pi2paddress.b32.i2p,127.0.0.1:18085
            tx-proxy=i2p,127.0.0.1:4447,disable_noise
            ```

            Replace `yourp2pi2paddress.b32.i2p` with the P2P tunnel's address. Do not put the RPC address in `anonymous-inbound`.

        5. The node is now available on I2P. You can check that the service is working by using `curl`:

            ``` Bash
            curl -x socks5h://127.0.0.1:4447 http://yourrpci2paddress.b32.i2p:18089/get_info
            ```
